# Project agent guidance

## Purpose and status

Build an offline standalone Windows x64 converter from 12daz/12da to editable 2D DXF. Version 0.1.3 implements scanning, supported 2D DXF conversion and a desktop GUI. See docs/validation_status.md for measured evidence and pending release checks. Do not describe unverified cases as passed.

## Source of truth

- readme.md: overview and current status.
- 12daz_design.md: architecture and conversion behaviour.
- docs/test_requirements.md: test requirements, cases and release gates.
- docs/implementation_steps.md: ordered implementation tasks and completion criteria.
- docs/validation_status.md: current evidence and remaining qualification.
- docs/assets/12daz-converter-gui-proposal.png: visual proposal, not running software.

## Agreed scope

Use Python, Tk/ttk and ezdxf for the initial implementation. Tk replaces the proposed PySide6 toolkit; keep conversion independent of the UI. Package the runtime so end users do not need Python, AutoCAD or 12d Model. DXF 2018 is the initial output. DWG is future work. Preserve full survey XY coordinates and flatten geometry to Z=0. Unit declaration must not silently rescale coordinates. Report unsupported content and approximations.

## Implementation guidance

Separate archive reading, grammar parsing, geometry conversion, DXF writing and GUI code. Parse documented grammar and validate against real fixtures. Stream archive input, bound resource usage, and keep the UI responsive through a worker process. Never substitute mockup counts or geometry for parsed data. Publish validated output atomically and protect input and existing destination files on failure or cancellation.

## Testing and reporting

Use the case IDs and acceptance criteria in docs/test_requirements.md. Add meaningful tests alongside implemented behaviour; do not create passing placeholders for missing features. Run relevant checks for each change and report what actually ran. Keep independent CAD interoperability and clean-machine packaging checks as release requirements. Record benchmark hardware, total process-tree memory and elapsed time before making performance claims.

## Repository hygiene

Keep private survey fixtures in ignored sample/ and generated conversion output in ignored outputs/. Commit synthetic fixtures only with redistribution permission; update ignore rules narrowly if needed for approved fixture paths. Do not commit secrets, environments, build output or private surveys. Preserve user changes and keep documentation aligned with implemented scope.

## GUI theme and preview

Preserve the dark grey theme, blue primary actions and three-panel layout. Share the 30,000-point preview allowance across selected models and regenerate it in the background after selection changes. Never apply a stale preview job result or limit DXF output to display geometry.

## GUI help

Implement a Help menu with bundled offline User Guide, About, and License and Third-party Notices. About must show the installed version/build and the exact notice: Copyright (c) 2026 Lambda Technologies Pty Ltd ABN 22 647 165 160. Keep notices consistent with LICENSE and test UI-07/UI-08.

## Optional support

Use only the owner-supplied public support URL, https://paypal.me/12dazapp. Support is optional. Show the brief dismissible splash only for normal GUI startup; never open the browser automatically or show the splash in CLI/worker/smoke-test paths. Keep the same link in Help > About and the README.

## License

Project source and documentation use the MIT license in LICENSE. Preserve third-party license notices. This project license does not grant redistribution rights to third-party dependencies or private input data.
